    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n- [X] I have updated 'mini.nvim' to latest version\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.files\n\n### Description\n\nUsing the plugin together with `mini.tabline` and `mini.bufremove`, all installed and loaded using `lazy.nvim` package manager.\r\n\r\nClosing a buffer using `MiniBufremove.delete()` or `:bd` either from a key mapping or direction execution from the command line, then reopening said buffer using `mini.files` results in the buffer not being registered as a listed buffer. Unsure if this is intended behavior or not. The buffer is displayed fine in the current window as the active buffer.\r\n\r\nThe main annoyance that occurs is with the tabline. The tabline displays incorrectly for the current buffer, as it continues to label it with the previous buffer (now \"covered\" by the new reopened buffer), despite showing the contents of the new reopened buffer. If the behavior is intended, will highly appreciate it if a solution can be shared for smoother integration ㅠㅠ.\n\n### Neovim version\n\nNVIM v0.9.1\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n1. Install `mini.files`, `mini.tabline`, and `mini.bufremove`, unconfigured.\r\n2. Open multiple buffers.\r\n3. Close a buffer, using `:bd` or `:lua MiniBufremove.delete()`.\r\n4. Open the closed buffer using `mini.files`.\r\n\n\n### Expected behavior\n\n![expected](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/assets/129026439/7d6a82c0-7454-4cea-a2f7-d49e245b158e)\n\n### Actual behavior\n\n![actual](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/assets/129026439/cbf4492c-bdab-442e-810c-039b95615123)\r\n",
