        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for taking time to create this!\r\n\r\nYes, this is an intended behavior. Opening a file first tries to look for an existing buffer for the target path. In case of both `MiniBufremove.bdelete()` and `:bdelete`, the file isn't *actually deleted* (it is removed from buffer list and some data related to it is cleared, see `:h :bdelete`), so it is reused by 'mini.files'. To verify that it is actually present after `:bd`, execute `:buffers!` instead of `:buffers`: that buffer should have `u` flag next to it meaning it is \"unlisted\". It is not made \"listed\" again solely because I can see people actually preferring for file explorer to not update existing buffers when not asked to.\r\n\r\nTo *actually delete* a buffer, use `MiniBufremove.wipeout()` or `:bw`.\r\n\r\nClosing as this is by design.",
